  "description": "Five 15 MW frame-class gas turbines (all available, none started at t=0) backed by four 6 MW solar feeders (24 MW total) and a 15 MW / 7.5 MWh grid-forming BESS. Location: San Diego, CA (SDG&E territory, 60 Hz WECC, solar anchored at 12:00 PST = UTC 20). GPU/data traffic starts at 800 nodes (8.4 MW gross) and increases exponentially every 3 minutes over 30 minutes, reaching 3,200 nodes (33.6 MW) — 400% of baseline (4×). Traffic holds at peak for 30 minutes (t=1800–3600 s), then declines exponentially over 30 minutes back to baseline. Total run: 3 hours (10,800 s). Solar irradiance declines from noon through mid-afternoon (0.88 → 0.82 → 0.70). At baseline the 24 MW solar array covers load entirely; as demand surpasses solar output (≈ t=1200 s) the 15 MW BESS absorbs the gap; at peak (12.5 MW net gap) the BESS can bridge ~36 minutes — enough for one cold-start turbine (15 min sync + ramp) if the operator acts promptly. N−1 firm capacity is zero until at least two turbines are synchronised. Operators must decide when to start each of the five available units as demand grows and solar declines through the afternoon.",

